<article class="ed-guide bg-canvas py-12 md:py-16" aria-label="Printer prints but will not scan"><div class="ed-layout">
{{ contents([{"id": "start", "title": "Find your starting point"}, {"id": "scanner-capability", "title": "Confirm what the device supports"}, {"id": "scan-settings", "title": "Check the scan application"}, {"id": "safe-scan-test", "title": "Run one safe scan"}, {"id": "related", "title": "What to read next"}]) }}
<div class="ed-reading guide-reading text-lg">
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A multifunction printer can print successfully while scanning is unavailable, unsupported or pointed at the wrong device. Treat printing and scanning as separate observations. First establish whether the equipment includes a scanner, then check the software and source selection before trying a small, non-confidential scan.</p>
<section class="ed-start" id="start" aria-labelledby="start-heading"><p class="ed-label">Start here</p><h2 id="start-heading">Separate the two jobs</h2><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Use the printer, computer and scan application as three clues rather than assuming one working function proves the others.</p><div class="ed-choices">
{{ choice("Confirm scanner capability", "Check the model documentation or visible hardware to confirm that the device is intended to scan, not only print.", "#scanner-capability", "startup", "/img/guides/printer-field-guide-v1.webp", "A household printer with a sheet of paper.", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Check the selected scanner", "Open the supported scan application and confirm the intended scanner, source and file type.", "#scan-settings", "applications", "/img/guides/printer-field-guide-v1.webp", "A laptop and connection cable.", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Test and reopen the file", "Make one harmless sample scan, save it locally and open it to check whether the result is usable.", "#safe-scan-test", "online", "/img/guides/printer-field-guide-v1.webp", "A magnifying glass over a printed test page.", "Read this part") }}
</div></section>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="scanner-capability"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>Confirm what the device supports</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Printing and scanning use different hardware and software paths. A printer that prints is not automatically a scanner. Check the exact model and its manufacturer information, or look for the scanner glass, document feeder or scan controls. Do not infer support from the printer name alone.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A local copy may show that the scanner hardware can operate, but it does not prove that scanning to a particular computer, folder or online destination is configured.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="scan-settings"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Check the scan application</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">On Windows, the Windows Scan application can be used when supported. Select the intended scanner, choose the source and file type, review the available options, then use the preview or scan control where offered. The <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/hardware/printer/install-and-use-a-scanner-in-windows">Microsoft Windows scanner guidance</a> describes these choices and saving the result.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Use the exact device and application that you intend to keep using. If the scanner is missing from the list, record the model and software name rather than installing several unrelated packages.</p>{% call note("care", "Take care", "Protect the test material") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Use a blank page or other non-confidential sample. Do not test by sending private documents to email or an unfamiliar online service.</p>{% endcall %}
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="safe-scan-test"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Run one safe scan</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Place a harmless sample on the glass or in the supported feeder. Scan once, save the result to a known local folder and reopen it. Check whether the file exists, opens and shows the expected page. Note the selected source and file type if the result is blank, cropped or unreadable.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If a local copy works but scanning from the computer does not, keep those as separate observations. If neither works, compare the exact error with the device model and application. A working print connection does not establish scan-to-email or every other scan feature.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Keep the result, error and selected settings together for the next practical step. Avoid repeated setup changes until the exact scanner support and software path are known.</p>
